    "M07-positive-control-wrong-hold": {
      "decides_nothing": true,
      "green": true,
      "measured_cells": {
        "corpus-noreserve": "KILLED"
      },
      "replaced_by": "P01-positive-control-accepted-hold-too-large",
      "retirement_reason": "Arm B's M07 is HP-06's declared BROADER-REACH SUBSTITUTE: it inflates the\nCOMPUTATION of the held total rather than the amount recorded on an accepted\nreservation. On this arm `available()` is derived on every read, so the fault is\npresent from construction, on every tenant, after any command including a\nrefusal -- which is a different mutant from the sealed semantic (\"breaks on the\nvery first accepted reservation\") and a different experiment from arm A's M07.\n\nThe consequence is the one that matters for a CONTROL. `oracle.py` states M07's\npurpose: if `Reserve` stops executing, M07 must go red. Arm A's does. Arm B's is\nkilled by `CloseTenant` cases on states with no live reservation, so it stays\ngreen through exactly the regression HP-06 shipped -- and arm B's catalogue\ncarries arm A's role string over it verbatim, \"must die on every instrument that\ncan execute an accepted Reserve\", which arm B's own data contradicts.\n\nM07 is NOT deleted, NOT re-seeded and NOT excused. It still runs, it is still\nscored in the `wrong_value` class row, and its kills stand as measurements. What\nit stops doing is deciding whether the instrument works.\n\nIts `corpus-slice-led` limitation is separately rejected by the driver, on arm\nB's own numbers, because `corpus-neg` kills M07 while executing zero accepted\n`Reserve` cases -- the very condition that limitation calls undecidable.",
      "role": "positive (RETIRED)"
    },
    "M09-negative-control-ledger-order": {
      "decides_nothing": true,
      "green": true,
      "measured_cells": {
        "corpus-noreserve": "KILLED"
      },
      "replaced_by": "N01-negative-control-outstanding-id-order",
      "retirement_reason": "Retired for the reason given in full in examples/validation/ab/eval/controls.toml:\nM09 reverses a SEQUENCE and this model represents its ledger as one\n(`ledger' = Append(ledger, ...)`, projected as a tuple and compared\npositionally), so ordering is expressible and the corpus sees it. The retirement\nis a property of the MODEL, so it holds on every tree measured against that\nmodel. M09 is not deleted, not re-seeded and not excused: it still runs and is\nstill scored in the `ordering` class row. What it stops doing is deciding\nwhether the instrument works.",
      "role": "negative (RETIRED)"
    },
